## 6. The Old-to-Young Problem: Card Tables and Write Barriers

A young collection must find every young object that is live, including those referenced **only** by old objects:

```java
class Cache {
    static Holder holder = new Holder();  // old after many GCs
}
class Holder {
    Object value;
}

// Later:
Cache.holder.value = new Object();  // old object now points to a young object
```

Scanning the entire old generation on every minor GC would remove the benefit of generations. Instead, the JVM tracks old-to-young pointers using a **card table**.

- The old generation is divided into **cards** (512 bytes in HotSpot).
- A byte array has one entry per card.
- Whenever a reference field is written, the JIT emits a **write barrier** that marks the card dirty.

```
// Conceptual post-write barrier emitted by the JIT for: obj.field = value
obj.field = value;
card_table[(address_of(obj)) >> 9] = DIRTY;
```

During a minor GC, only dirty cards are scanned as extra roots. G1 extends this with per-region **remembered sets** that record which regions point into each region, so it can collect any subset of regions independently.

Write barriers are a real throughput cost. Concurrent collectors add even more barrier work (see section 9).

