## 9. Concurrent Marking and Why Barriers Are Necessary

When marking runs while the application is mutating the graph, an object can be lost. The classic failure occurs when both of these happen:

1. The application stores a reference to a white object into a black object (already scanned).
2. The application removes all paths to that white object from gray objects.

```java
// GC has scanned 'a' (black), has not yet scanned 'b' (gray).
// 'c' is white, reachable only via b.ref.

a.ref = b.ref;   // black a now points to white c
b.ref = null;    // gray b no longer points to c

// The GC finishes scanning b, never sees c, and frees it.
// But 'a' still points to c -> use-after-free.
```

Collectors break one of the two conditions:

- **Incremental update** (CMS): a write barrier records stores into black objects so they get rescanned.
- **Snapshot-at-the-beginning** (G1, Shenandoah): a pre-write barrier records the old value being overwritten, so everything live at the start of marking stays marked.

This is why concurrent collectors cost throughput: every reference store, and for ZGC and Shenandoah every reference load, includes extra instructions.

