## 13. Putting It Together: An Experiment

This program creates a mix of short-lived and long-lived data. Run it with different collectors and compare the logs.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class GcWorkload {
    // Long-lived data: simulates a cache that slowly turns over
    private static final Map<Integer, byte[]> CACHE = new LinkedHashMap<>(16, 0.75f, true) {
        @Override protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry<Integer, byte[]> e) {
            return size() > 20_000;  // ~200 MB of old-gen data
        }
    };

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Random rnd = new Random(42);
        long start = System.nanoTime();
        long checksum = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < 5_000_000; i++) {
            // Short-lived garbage: dies immediately
            String s = "request-" + i + "-" + rnd.nextInt();
            checksum += s.hashCode();

            // Occasional long-lived allocation
            if (i % 50 == 0) {
                CACHE.put(rnd.nextInt(100_000), new byte[10_240]);
            }
        }

        System.out.printf("Done in %d ms, checksum=%d%n",
            (System.nanoTime() - start) / 1_000_000, checksum);
    }
}
```

```bash
java -Xmx1g -XX:+UseSerialGC   -Xlog:gc:file=serial.log   GcWorkload
java -Xmx1g -XX:+UseParallelGC -Xlog:gc:file=parallel.log GcWorkload
java -Xmx1g -XX:+UseG1GC       -Xlog:gc:file=g1.log       GcWorkload
java -Xmx1g -XX:+UseZGC        -Xlog:gc:file=zgc.log      GcWorkload
```

Typical observations:

- **Serial and Parallel**: the fewest total GC CPU cycles, but the longest individual pauses, especially for any full GCs as the cache fills old gen.
- **G1**: young pauses of a few to tens of milliseconds, plus concurrent marking and mixed collections as old occupancy grows.
- **ZGC**: pauses well under a millisecond, but more GC threads competing for CPU and somewhat lower raw throughput.

Exact numbers depend on hardware, core count, and JDK version, so measure on your own system.

